================================================================================================
CROSS-CLASS SUMMARY: AUTO-mode max range vs. site turbulence condition
================================================================================================
Cn2 class     Max range (km)    Loss @ max (dB)   
weak          291.63            91.62             
moderate      291.63            91.62             
strong        288.29            90.76             

Interpretation:
- 'forced' rows show what each single model reports even past its own validity
  boundary for that Cn2 -- included to show how much a misapplied model over/
  understates range, not as a recommended operating mode.
- AUTO is the physically appropriate estimate at every distance for a given site's
  Cn2, switching models at the regime boundaries listed above.
- Fading can LENGTHEN the max range beyond the deterministic cutoff (251.5 km): R(eta) is convex near the
  cutoff, so rare upward fades lift the ergodic average. Here Gamma-Gamma (forced) gives 291.6 km / 291.6 km / 291.6 km for weak / moderate / strong Cn2.
- Log-Normal forced outside its validity (arm sigma_R^2 > 1) is NOT a prediction: moderate 124.3 km, strong 10.5 km (weak value 300.0 km is the sweep limit, i.e. not a true maximum).
- Gamma-Gamma saturates (scintillation index -> ~1), so with plain Gamma-Gamma the max range is nearly
  Cn2-independent. Turbulence beam broadening (v2.3, example 07) is what makes range fall with Cn2.
- 'Corrected' / AUTO strong-class rows include aperture averaging with small pointing jitter and beam wander.
- Numerical note: this example uses 20,000-sample Monte Carlo per point on a 180-point grid (coarser than
  example 06's quadrature + bisection), so max ranges agree with example 06 only to ~0.1-1 km.
